<title>SOUTH KOREA: Daewoo Motor union returns to work on Tuesday.</title>
<headline>Daewoo Motor union returns to work on Tuesday.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1996-12-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>Daewoo Motor Co Ltd workers returned to work on Tuesday after a three-day strike protesting against the passage of a controversial labour law, a union official said.</p>
<p>The union planned to hold a meeting on Friday to decided whether to resume industrial action, he said.  </p>
<p>Union workers at Daewoo Motor, a unit of South Korea's</p>
<p>&quot;We've decided to return to work in response to the Korean Confederation of Trade Union's call to postpone strikes till next year,&quot; said a union official who declined to be named.</p>
<p>&quot;We will gather on January 3 to decide whether or not to start the strike up again,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>January 1 and 2 are public holidays in South Korea.</p>
<p>-- Seoul Newsroom (822) 727 5644</p>
